CHAPTERS={"3":{"title":"Hot keys, stampedes & negative results","intro":"Imagine a supermarket price board that is copied to many counters. A price changes at headquarters, but a shopper might still see the old label. In this chapter, hot keys, stampedes & negative results becomes a practical problem to solve. Begin with the user's expectation before choosing any technology.","lesson":[{"topic":"Hot keys.","steps":["Begin with the smallest arrangement that provides the user-visible outcome.","Change one requirement or fail one component to expose the new engineering decision.","Compare the simplest sufficient solution with the extra complexity and test the actual contract."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Hot keys.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"Cache stampedes, penetration, and avalanche.","steps":["The first read fetches the original value; a later read may reuse the copy.","A changed original, expired entry, or burst of cache misses can make that copy wrong or expensive.","Test simultaneous readers, updates, expiration, and whether the answer must be current."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Cache stampedes, penetration, and avalanche.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"Negative caching.","steps":["Begin with the smallest arrangement that provides the user-visible outcome.","Change one requirement or fail one component to expose the new engineering decision.","Compare the simplest sufficient solution with the extra complexity and test the actual contract."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Negative caching.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"Stale-while-revalidate.","steps":["The first read fetches the original value; a later read may reuse the copy.","A changed original, expired entry, or burst of cache misses can make that copy wrong or expensive.","Test simultaneous readers, updates, expiration, and whether the answer must be current."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Stale-while-revalidate.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"Cache warming and prefetching.","steps":["The first read fetches the original value; a later read may reuse the copy.","A changed original, expired entry, or burst of cache misses can make that copy wrong or expensive.","Test simultaneous readers, updates, expiration, and whether the answer must be current."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Cache warming and prefetching.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."}],"code":"def read_product(key, cache, db):\n    value = cache.get(key)\n    if value is None:\n        value = db.get(key)\n        if value is not None: cache.set(key, value)\n    return value  # freshness policy is a separate contract","diagram":["contrast","ladder","timeline","boundary","tree"]},"4":{"title":"Edges, partitions & the price of a cache","intro":"Imagine a supermarket price board that is copied to many counters. A price changes at headquarters, but a shopper might still see the old label. In this chapter, edges, partitions & the price of a cache becomes a practical problem to solve. Begin with the user's expectation before choosing any technology.","lesson":[{"topic":"Redis-like data structures.","steps":["Begin with the smallest arrangement that provides the user-visible outcome.","Change one requirement or fail one component to expose the new engineering decision.","Compare the simplest sufficient solution with the extra complexity and test the actual contract."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Redis-like data structures.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"CDN architecture and origin shielding.","steps":["The first read fetches the original value; a later read may reuse the copy.","A changed original, expired entry, or burst of cache misses can make that copy wrong or expensive.","Test simultaneous readers, updates, expiration, and whether the answer must be current."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"CDN architecture and origin shielding.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"Cache partitioning and replication.","steps":["The first read fetches the original value; a later read may reuse the copy.","A changed original, expired entry, or burst of cache misses can make that copy wrong or expensive.","Test simultaneous readers, updates, expiration, and whether the answer must be current."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"Cache partitioning and replication.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."},{"topic":"When caching is harmful.","steps":["Begin with the smallest arrangement that provides the user-visible outcome.","Change one requirement or fail one component to expose the new engineering decision.","Compare the simplest sufficient solution with the extra complexity and test the actual contract."],"question":"In this worked system, explain \"When caching is harmful.\" with a before-and-after state. Which actor owns the decision? What would a retry, a crash, or a sudden traffic spike change? Identify one measurable test before adding complexity."}],"code":"def read_product(key, cache, db):\n    value = cache.get(key)\n    if value is None:\n        value = db.get(key)\n        if value is not None: cache.set(key, value)\n    return value  # freshness policy is a separate contract","diagram":["state","ledger","sequence","decision"]}}
